The ability to self-propel – seen in animals, individual cells, and microorganisms – has inspired the design of a wide range of artificial systems capable of autonomous locomotion. Examples include droplets (sometimes tactic1-5) driven by interfacial reactions, segmented nanorods6,7 powered by the catalytic decomposition of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , micromotors fueled by catalytic reactions,8-10 light,11,12 electric13,14 and magnetic15,16 fields, polymer capsules,17,18 exfoliating particles,19 as well as numerous forms of the so-called camphor boats based on gels20-23 or polymers.24 In particular, camphor boats spread surface-active chemicals onto the interface at which they rest thus establishing surface tension gradients, which, in turn, set up convective Marangoni flows in the surrounding fluid.25-27 These flows then power the boats to perform different types of motion (continuous,28 oscillatory,21 or intermittent29,30) and, if many boats are present, can drive formation of dynamic structures, including open-lattice arrays31 or swarms in which smaller particles assemble behind and follow larger “leaders”.32 Recent progress in the synthesis of metal-organic framework, MOF, crystals33 and films34 of appreciable dimensions (i.e., larger than microscopic crystallites) has prompted interest in these materials also acting as autonomous movers – this idea rests on the assumption that MOFs’ highly ordered porous structures could
## ACS Nano
regulate release of surface-active molecules and thus control the ensuing motion. Pioneering examples of self-propelled MOFs come from the Matsui group35-37 but the diphenylalanine “fuel” used is costly (ca. $\$500/\mathrm{g}$ from Sigma Aldrich) and the particle shape is not controlled. Here, we describe a different self-propelled MOF – based on freestanding films34 of the porphyrin-based porous coordination network P $\mathrm{\boldmath~\cdot~}222^{38-42}.$ – that not only uses much less expensive fuels (e.g., benzoic acid, dimethylformamide, diethylformamide, dibutylformamide) but also combines several other interesting attributes: (i) The moving particles cut from large MOF films can have arbitrary shapes, including those that ensure directionality of motion; (ii) The efficiency of motion (in terms of achievable velocities and kinetic energies) are significantly higher than in the majority of previously reported gel or MOF motors; (iii) the velocities of the MOF swimmers depend on the microstructure of the film which differs between its two sides; (iv) In later stages of their motion, the MOFs exhibit and can switch between two distinct patterns of motion (continuous vs. intermittent); and (v) the MOFs can be refueled multiple times; and (vi) When multiple particles are present at the interface, they organize into “open” structures that can move collectively and in time periodic manner.
The paper is organized as follows. First, we describe the microstructure of the porphyrin MOFs we use and the morphologies of their freestanding films. We then describe qualitatively the motility patterns exhibited by differently-shaped MOF particles and identify the chemical “fuels” these particles emit to power their motions – with this knowledge, we are able to quantify the efficiency of motion achieved with different fuels and relate it to the oriented architecture of MOF crystallites within the films. We then analyze the motility patterns for particles of different sizes and also in ensembles of interacting particles exhibiting collective behaviors.
Results and Discussion.
(i) Structure of the MOF films. The structure of the PCN-222 MOF $(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{8}\mathrm{L}_{3})$ ) we used is illustrated in Figure 1a and features 3.7 nm-wide channels and $1.2\ \mathrm{nm}$ cavities. It is constituted of hydrophobic meso-tetra(4-carboxyphenyl)porphyrin ( $\mathrm{\tilde{H}}_{4}$ -TCPP- $\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , L) ligands responsible for the framework’s hydrophobicity and Zr-oxo clusters binding the $\mathrm{H}_{4}$ -TCPP- $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ ligands. We have recently shown34 that using a simple solvo-thermal procedure (see Methods), PCN-222 can be grown on various supports into polycrystalline, ca. $100\mathrm{-}\upmu\mathrm{m}$ -thick films as large as six inches across (Figure 1b). Interestingly, on Cu supports, initial formation of Cu-benzoate paddle-wheel pre-complexes promotes directional growth such that the MOF films feature preferred crystalline orientation and have distinctly different microstructures and wettability on their two sides (see 34 for further details). At the side facing the Cu substrate (henceforth, the “substrate” side), the MOF crystalline domains are relatively tightly packed (Figure 1c) and the surface is hydrophilic; the “free” side presents a mesh of thin pillar-like microcrystallites (Figure 1d) and the surface is hydrophobic (see SI, Section 2). Unless noted otherwise, the experiments described are for the situation where the films were placed with the “free” side facing the water surface.
(ii) Individual MOF particles and their motions. Since the PCN-222 MOF films are freestanding and quite robust, it is possible to cut out from them pieces of various shapes, such as those shown in Figure 1e. When such particles are then placed on the surface of water, they move rapidly with speed up to $180\ \mathrm{mm^{\cdot}s^{-1}}$ . Circular disks perform random motions. Compact polygons (e.g., squares or equilateral triangles) typically move in circular orbits around the dish as illustrated in Figure 2a. Such particles tend to continue in the same direction, though the choice of the initial direction is random for different particles. More elongated shapes (e.g.,
## ACS Nano
slanted rectangular bars shown in Figure 2c and Movie S1) rotate rapidly about their center whereas asymmetric, V-shaped particles move spiked-end forward (Figure 2d and Movie S2). In all cases, the particles slow down with time and ultimately enter a regime of intermittent motions interspersed with periods of inactivity (Figure 2b). Previously we32 and others28,29 observed all of such motion patterns for similarly shaped camphor boats – this analogy suggests that the motions of the MOFs we study here also originate from surface tension effects due to an emitted “fuel”.
(iii) The nature of chemical fuels. If the analogy to camphor boats is to hold, the boats must emit surface active chemicals onto the water-air interface. There are potentially two candidates, both used during MOF synthesis: (1) Benzoic acid, BA, acting as a coordination and crystallization modulator (decreasing coordination strength between Zr nodes and $\mathrm{H}_{4}$ -TCPP- $\mathbf{{\cdotH}}_{2}$ ligands43 and thus increasing reversibility). BA has low water solubility and is relatively hydrophobic,44 and when its pellets are placed onto water surface, they also move, confirming that dissolution of BA can modulate surface tension. (2) Dimethylformamide, DMF, solvent which is miscible with water but with density $(0.945\ \mathrm{g}^{.}\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ at $293\mathrm{~K~}$ ) and surface tension (γ $=3.71\cdot10^{-2}\ \mathrm{N}{\cdot}\mathrm{m}^{-1})$ both less than water, suggesting that it could also behave as a surfactant if emitted from the MOF.
To confirm that BA and DMF were both emitted from the MOF, we placed the MOF pieces into a Petri dish filled with $20~\mathrm{mL}$ of deuterated water, $\mathrm{D}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ . After 20 minutes, we removed the MOF, concentrated the $\mathrm{D}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ to $5\%$ of its original volume, and then acquired $^1\mathrm{H}$ NMR spectra, which clearly showed characteristic signals of both BA and DMF (see SI, Section 3). We also visualized flows driven by gradients of surface tension by holding the particles stationary while immersed in dye-colored water. The streaming, outward flows are captured in
## ACS Nano
Figure S7. Taken together, the above experiments confirm that the MOFs are powered by the emission of BA and DMF.
Next, we investigated whether fuels other than BA/DMF mixture could be used and what speeds they stimulate. To this end, we prepared “activated” films without BA or DMF that were then loaded with other volatile solvents. The guest-free MOFs were analyzed for nitrogen adsorption (Figure 3) allowing us to estimate the surface area of the guest-free MOF as $\sim2350$ $\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ which agrees with previous reports.34 The activated MOFs thus prepared were soaked in various solvents (see SI, Section 5) and, to ensure that no liquids remained adsorbed on the MOFs surface, dried for 3 hours at a temperature $10~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ above the boiling point of the solvent being encapsulated. For the series of experiments and comparisons described below, the MOF films were cut into pieces having the same shapes $5\mathrm{~x~}5\mathrm{~mm}$ squares).
First we compare swimming of the original MOF loaded with DMF and BA, to that of samples housing only DMF. Figure 4a shows that the former particle initially moves in a continuous fashion with speed rapidly decreasing from ${\sim}120~\mathrm{mm^{\cdot}s^{-1}}$ to about $10\ \mathrm{mm^{\cdot}s^{-1}}$ after ${\sim}600~\mathrm{s}$ . At later times, the nature of the motion changes and the particle spends most of the time immobile with sporadic rapid “jumps” discussed below. When the similarly shaped particle is loaded with only DMF, its motion (Figure 4b) is qualitatively similar but the initial speed is slower $\mathrm{(\sim75\mm^{\cdot}s^{-1}},$ ) and transition from continuous to intermittent motion takes place sooner (t $\sim250~\mathrm{s)}$ . Although it would be tempting to ascribe these differences to the presence or absence of BA in the two types of particles, we note that direct comparison is complicated by different contents of the fuels $(\sim35~\mathrm{wt\%}$ of BA/DMF vs. 15 wt% for DMF alone) determined by thermogravimetric analysis, TGA (Figures 4c and 4d). What these experiments establish clearly, however, is that DMF itself can power the MOFs.
## ACS Nano
Next, we investigated structurally-related formamide derivatives: diethylformamide, DEF, and dibutylformamide, DBF. Using the former, we achieved speeds of $160\ \mathrm{mm^{\cdot}s^{-1}}$ and using the latter, speeds of $210\mathrm{mm^{\cdot}s^{-1}}$ . Speeds appear to increase with increasing length of the alkyl chains, increasing from DMF, to DEF, to DBF. This trend can be attributed to (i) the enhanced ability of more hydrophobic molecules to be incorporated into the MOFs’ hydrophobic interior – as, indeed, confirmed by TGA analyses summarized in Figure S10; and (ii) the fact that DBF is not miscible in water and its surface tension is the lowest in the series $(2.94\cdot10^{-2}\ \mathrm{N}\cdot\mathrm{m}^{-1}\ \nu s.\ 3.29\cdot10^{-2}$ $\mathrm{{Nm}^{-1}}$ for DEF and $3.71{\cdot}10^{-2}\mathrm{N}{\cdot}\mathrm{m}^{-1}$ for DMF; see measurements in the SI, Section 6). We also tested many other solvents (various alcohols, tetrahydrofuran, chloroform, ethylene glycol, DMSO, acetone). Among these, only THF and acetone were able to cause MOF motion and only for short times. These observations show that propulsion depends on more subtle considerations than just density and surface tension (cf. Table S1 in Section 5). We consider that the amount loaded into the MOF and the fuel’s miscibility with water also influence the efficacy of spreading at the water-air interface and the lifetime during which the particles remain activelypropelled.
(iv) The efficiency of motions powered by different fuels. It is instructive to compare the “efficiency” of motion of these MOFs to those of MOFs in prior literature. Such comparisons are typically performed by considering (i) the maximum speed per volume of the moving particle (here, quantity denoted as $\upsilon_{m a x,}$ ); and (ii) the particle’s maximum kinetic energy per unit mass of expended “fuel,” $\varepsilon_{K,m a x}$ . For the HKUST-1 MOF in reference,3 $\mathrm{{^7}\upsilon_{\it m a x}=45\ m m^{-2}{\cdot}s^{-1}}$ whereas for CuJAST-1 MOF in reference,36 $\upsilon_{m a x}=67~\mathrm{mm^{-2}{\cdot}s^{-1}}$ . For the $5\ \mathrm{mm}\times5\ \mathrm{mm}\times0.1\ \mathrm{mn}$ PCN-222 MOF particles we used, the values of $\upsilon_{m a x}$ are $48~\mathrm{mm^{-2}\cdot s^{-1}}$ with BA/DMF fuel but as high as 84 $\mathrm{mm}^{-2}\cdot\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ for DBF fuel. Still, it is fair to say that the characteristics of all these MOFs are
## ACS Nano
comparable. For our system, however, the kinetic energy measure of efficiency is significantly higher. With the $6.1~\mathrm{mg}$ mass of a $5\ \mathrm{mm}\ \times\ 5\ \mathrm{mm}\ \times\ 0.1\ \mathrm{mm}$ particle and taking the TGAestablished (maximum possible) fuel loss at $35\mathrm{\wt\%}$ , the values of $\varepsilon_{K,m a x}$ are $22~\upmu\mathrm{J}\cdot\upunderline{{\mathbf{g}}}^{-1}$ when such a particle is powered by BA/DMF (maximum velocity $120\ \mathrm{mm^{\cdot}s^{-1}},$ ) and $57.7~\upmu\mathrm{J}^{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ when it is powered by DBF (maximum velocity $210\ \mathrm{mm^{\cdot}s^{-1}}$ ). These values compare favorably with DPApowered CuJAST- $\cdot1^{36}$ MOFs $(4\upmu\mathrm{J}\cdot\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ , or DPA-powered HKUST- $1~\mathrm{MOFs}^{37}(3.97~\upmu\mathrm{J}^{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}).$ .
The improved performance of PCN-222 can be reasonably explained by differences in this MOF’s microstructure. Calculations using the PLATON software45 give the single-crystal density of guest-free PCN-222 of $0.49\ \mathrm{g}\cdot\mathrm{cm}^{-3}.$ – this is less than for CuJAST- $^{46}\cdot^{(0.83\mathrm{~g}\cdot\mathrm{cm}^{-3})}$ or HKUST- $1^{47}\ (1.90\ \mathrm{g}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-3})$ , and reflects large mesoporosity of PCN-222. At the same time, the solvent accessible volume per unit cell volume is $79.4\%$ $(20750\mathrm{~}\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{3}/26149\mathrm{~}\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{3})$ for PCN-222 but only $61.3\%$ for CuJAST- $1^{46}$ and $60\%$ for HKUST- $1^{48}$ – these values can correlate with the theoretical “fuel capacity” of the MOFs and ultimately translate into their kinetic-energy characteristics.
Comparing with non-MOFs materials infused with chemical “fuels,” the maximum reported22 velocity of camphor particles on the air/water interface was $60\ \mathrm{mm^{\cdot}s^{-1}}$ $(\upsilon_{m a x}=7.84~\mathrm{mm^{-2}\cdot s^{-1}})$ and lasting only few seconds, whereas that of a this agarose disks loaded with camphor31 was around 20 $\mathrm{mm^{\cdot}s^{-1}}$ $(\upsilon_{m a x}=50.6~\mathrm{mm^{-2}\cdot s^{-1}})$ . Impressive speeds ${\sim}300~\mathrm{mm^{\cdot}s^{-1}}$ and $\upsilon_{m a x}=25~\mathrm{mm^{-2}{\cdot}s^{-1}}$ vs. ${\sim}200$ $\mathrm{m}\mathrm{m}^{\cdot}\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ and $\upsilon_{m a x}=84~\mathrm{mm^{-2}\cdot s^{-1}}$ in our system) were achieved with lithographically structured poly-Nisopropylacrylamide (PNIPAm) gels soaked in ethanol and reported by the Gracias group.23 Similar to our MOFs, larger PNIPAm particles moved slower but for longer times; unlike the mechanically sturdy MOFs, however, such large and thin gel pieces tended to buckle resulting in more chaotic motion patterns. Beyond gels, polymer capsules from Pumera’s laboratory have been shown17,18 to move with velocities up to $150\ \mathrm{mm^{\cdot}s^{-1}}$ $(\upsilon_{m a x}=8.96~\mathrm{mm^{-2}\cdot s^{-1}})$ , though without directional control.
