Recently, disks ( $7~\mathrm{mm}$ in radius, $0.055~\mathrm{{mm}}$ thick) of cellulose acetate imbibed with peppermint oil were shown24 to move on water with velocities up to $150\mathrm{mm^{\cdot}s^{-1}}$ and $\upsilon_{m a x}=17.73~\mathrm{mm^{-2}{\cdot}s^{-1}}$ . For these particles, the content of the peppermint-oil fuel was also measured $\mathrm{(6.7~mg}$ in a $10~\mathrm{mg}$ particle) allowing for the estimation of $\varepsilon_{K,m a x}\sim0.023~{\upmu\mathrm{J}}{\cdot}{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}-$ that is, significantly lower than for our MOFs or for several gel systems.
(v) Effects of microstructure on motility. The microstructure of the MOF films is also an important factor. When MOF pieces were placed on water with the tightly-packed, hydrophobic side (see Figure 1c) facing down, the velocities were much smaller $\mathrm{(<15\mm"s^{-1}}$ ), the efficiencies significantly lower $\mathrm{\Delta}\left({\upsilon}_{m a x}\sim6\mathrm{\mm}^{-2}\mathrm{s}^{-1}\right.$ , $\varepsilon_{K,m a x}\sim0.32~\upmu\mathrm{J}^{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ , and total duration of motion shorter (at most 1000 s) than for the same particles but with the hydrophilic, “pillared” (Figure 1d) side facing water. This difference can be explained by the array of crystalline pillars presenting a much larger surface area through which the fuel can be delivered to the surrounding medium more efficiently than from a flat surface.
The importance of pillars is further corroborated by studies in which the particles were refueled with DBF and reused after they came to a complete stop. The powder XRD spectra shown in Figure 5a do not show pronounced differences for the “fresh” particles and those refueled/reused 15 times. However, we observed that particle speeds decreased from $210\ \mathrm{mm^{\cdot}s^{-1}}$ on the first run to ca. $150\ \mathrm{mm^{\cdot}s^{-1}}$ on the fifteenth run (Figure 5b). SEM images in Figure 5c evidence that in the reused MOFs, the pillars on the “free” side (cf. Figure 1d) appear degraded and, additionally, there are cracks visible on the “substrate” side. These changes are not surprising given that every run and refueling cycle involves mechanical manipulation of the MOF particles which can damage the microscopic pillar texture.
(vi) Effects of particle size. As should be expected, the parameters of motion change with particle size. We studied such effects in a series of experiments with BA/DMF-loaded square particles with edge length ranging from $L=3~\mathrm{mm}$ to $11~\mathrm{mm}$ . The results summarized in Figure 6 reveal that larger particles move more slowly, with initial velocities decreasing from $150\ \mathrm{mm^{\cdot}s^{-1}}$ for $3\mathrm{mm}\times3$ mm squares to $90\mathrm{mm^{\cdot}s^{-1}}$ for the $11\ \mathrm{mm}\times11\ \mathrm{mm}$ particles. This trend can be ascribed to the fact that while the mass of the particles increases as $L^{2}$ , the flux of the fuel emitted onto the interface from the sides of the particles scales only as $L$ . At the same time, the larger particles propel themselves continuously for longer times – for instance, $3\ \mathrm{mm}$ squares start exhibiting intermittent motions at $\sim300$ s whereas $11~\mathrm{mm}$ squares move continuously for $>$ 900 s. This effect, observed previously for camphor boats,29,30 can be explained by smaller particles running out of chemical fuel earlier than larger ones: When the fuel is delivered onto the interface very slowly, the particle stops moving, “waits” until accumulation of fresh fuel reaches a certain critical value, and then expands this fuel rapidly during intermittent motion until the next stop point, after which the above scenario repeats (for more thorough discussion of this mechanism, see 29,30).
One property that does not change with particle size is the directionality of motion. This is evidenced by the circular graphs in Figures 6a-d which plot the distance (radial coordinate) the particle travels with respect to arbitrary coordinates set up at each particle’s center of mass – as seen, the angle between particle’s center of mass and its direction of propulsion is maintained through most of the motion. The vast majority of direction changes occur during collisions with the wall of the container in the initial, very fast phase of motion and later, when the particle exhibits intermittent motions.
(vii) Collective motions in ensembles of particles. The last aspect of the system we studied concerns the motions in larger collections of particles. In this respect, the MOF particles generally resemble camphor boats in which particles (in deep enough layers of liquid32) interact
## ACS Nano
repulsively via convective flows they set up in the fluid31 and effectively try to “avoid” one another. One type of peculiar and interesting behavior we observed is for long MOF rectangles such as those shown in Figure 7a and Movie S3. Here, two rectangles orient perpendicular to the rim of the dish since this orientation minimizes convection-mediated repulsive forces between the particle and the wall (see ref 31 for theoretical details of such hydrodynamic repulsions). One of the particles then approaches the other, repels it, follows up (likely due to the attractive component of the hydrodynamic force we described in 32), comes to a halt, repels again, and the pair collectively moves around the circumference of the dish until fuel runs out. During most of this cyclical process, the amplitude of the “oscillations” – quantified in Figure 7b by the difference of the $\theta_{I}$ and $\theta_{2}$ angles the rectangles form with respect to each other – remains roughly constant. When the system is comprised of more than two particles, the entire ensemble moves like a caterpillar, contracting from one end and expanding from the other, and overall travelling around the perimeter of the dish (with sporadic changes in the direction of motion, see Figure 8 and Movie S4). We suggest that the key factor in this type of motions is small inertia of the lightweight MOF particles that can come to a full stop after “pushing” the neighboring particle away – this is in contrast to our previous experiments with more massive camphor boats which, upon “colliding” with other particles did not come to a halt but “rebounded” in the “opposite” direction. Analysis of such collective motions certainly merits a more detailed theoretical analysis which we will attempt in future work.
Conclusions.
In summary, we have described autonomous swimmers using porphyrin-based PCN-222 MOF particles. We believe the most appealing characteristics of this system are (i) the ability to control the motility patterns by the shapes of MOF particles and the microstructure of the MOF films, and (ii) the high efficiency of particles’ motions, as quantified by their kinetic energies per the mass of chemical fuels used. Such high kinetic energies can be useful in microgenerators transducing chemical to mechanical or electric energies.49,50 With autonomous movers based on MOFs, it is also possible to use various instrumental techniques common in MOF research to characterize the supramolecular structure, internal surface area, sorption characteristics, etc., of these particles – obtaining such detailed information with gels or polymers would be difficult. From a fundamental point of view, the ensembles of MOFs appear attractive for studying collective behaviors in ensembles of objects interacting by the chemicals they emit. These findings enhance the potential uses of MOFs by exploiting their distinctively well-defined porous character to achieve useful materials functions.
Methods.
Synthesis of PCN MOFs. All reagents and solvents used were purchased commercially as reagent-quality and, unless otherwise noted, were used as received without further purification: $\mathrm{H}_{4}$ -TCPP- $\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}$ $(>97~\%$ , TCI), $\mathrm{ZrCl_{4}}$ $(99.9\%$ , Sigma-Aldrich), benzoic acid $(>99.5\%$ , Sigma-Aldrich), N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF), N,N-diethylformamide (DEF) $(\geq99.8\%$ Fischer Scientific), and N,N-dibutylformamide (DBF) $99\ \%$ , Sigma-Aldrich). Substrates for large-area growth of PCN films were Cu foils (thickness $0.25~\mathrm{mm}$ , $99.98\%$ trace metals basis, Sigma-Aldrich).
The MOFs were prepared according to previously reported solvo-thermal synthetic methods.34,51 To prepare 6-inch, wafer-scale PCN-222 films on Cu, $\mathrm{ZrCl_{4}}$ $(6\mathrm{g})$ , ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{TCPP}}$ (2.4 g), benzoic acid $(162~\mathrm{g})$ and DMF $(480~\mathrm{mL})$ ) were dissolved in a Duran-glass Petri dish $20\ \mathrm{cm}$ in
## ACS Nano
diameter) by sonication for $30~\mathrm{min}$ . The reaction vessel was tightly sealed and heated at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 48 hrs to deposit the MOF. MOF films were cleaned by washing with copious amounts of DMF three times (causing pontaneous delamination).
Before re-charging with DMF, guest-free PCN-222 films were prepared by washing the as-synthesized PCN-222 with copious amounts of DMF three times and then heating at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in DMF for 24 hrs. The samples were then immersed in acetone for 48 hrs and dried at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 1 day under high vacuum ( $\mathrm{i}0^{-3}$ Torr). Guest free films were used for gas adsorption/desorption measurements. MOFs containing other desired solvents were prepared by immersing guest-free PCN-222 films in these solvents for 24 hrs and subsequently drying for 3 hrs at a temperature 10 $^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ above the boiling point of the solvent being encapsulated.
Powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) of PCN films. Powder XRD (PXRD) spectra of the PCN films were collected on a Rigaku Smartlab system and Rigaku Miniplex 600 D/tex equipped with a Cu-sealed tube $(\lambda{=}1.54178\mathrm{~}\mathrm{\AA}$ ). The following conditions were used: $40\mathrm{\kV}$ , 30 mA, step: 0.01 °, speed duration $=2$ deg/min (Rigaku Smartlab), and $40\ \mathrm{kV}\ 15\ \mathrm{mA}$ , step: $0.01^{\circ}$ , scan speed $=0.5$ deg/min (Rigaku Miniplex 600 D/tex).
${\bf\Pi^{1}H}.$ -NMR spectra of species diffusing from the MOF particles. Proton nuclear magnetic resonance spectra were recorded on a 400 MHz Bruker apparatus. Chemical shifts for protons were reported in parts per million (ppm) that referenced to residual protium in the NMR solvent ( $_\mathrm{D}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ : ppm). As-synthesized PCN-222 “swimmers” containing various solvents (DMFBA@PCN-222, DMF $@$ PCN-222, DEF $@$ PCN-222, DBF $@$ PCN-222) were placed onto the interface between deuterium oxide $(\mathrm{D}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ and air in a polystyrene Petri dish. After the particle stopped moving and was removed from the dish, $\mathrm{D}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ was concentrated by evaporation at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and further analyzed by $\mathrm{^{1}H N M R}$ to evidence the presence of the emitted DMF/BA, DMF, DEF,
or DBF “fuels”.
Preparation and observation of MOF “swimmers”. To cut circular particles, a commercially available cork borer (Sigma-Aldrich, #Z165220) was gently pressed against the MOF film. Square, triangle and rod-shaped particles were cut out of the MOF film using a XActo precision surgical knife (Sigma-Aldrich, #Z407704). MOF particles thus prepared were placed onto the surface of deionized (DI) water in transparent, polystyrene Petri dishes $\langle85~\mathrm{mm}$ inner diameter or $52\ \mathrm{mm}$ inner diameter, both $14\ \mathrm{mm}$ deep) plasma-treated (100W Femto Science Convance plasma cleaner) for $10~\mathrm{{min}}$ prior to experiments. The resulting movies were recorded as top-view video files at a frame rate of 120 frames per second using a high speed camera (Fastec Imaging TS4). The sequential images were binarized to extract particle motions, and the velocities were analyzed using house-written Matlab scripts.
Density Functional Theory (DFT) volume calculation of guest molecules. Molecular radius and volume were determined using ChemDraw 3D and Gaussian 03W (method: # B3PW91). The structures of guest molecules were first drawn in ChemDraw Ultra 14.0, and imported into Chem3D Ultra 14.0, and optimized by Molecular Mechanics 2 (MM2) method with minimum RMS Gradient set to 0.100. Further structural optimization was done by # B3PW91 DFT method, using optimized geometry output, # B3PW91.
Surface tension measurements. Surface tension values for DEF and DBF were obtained by the pendant drop method using the Surface Electro Optics (SEO) Phoenix 300 apparatus, at a temperature of $20~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with the relative humidity of air $\sim30–40\%$ . Shape of the droplet was recorded by a digital camera and the images obtained (Figure S8) were analyzed with ImageJ.52 For reference/calibration, we also measured the surface tension of DMF and the value agreed with previous reports.53
